{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2519 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b1e1946af30e7cc86429b2114f86042fcf5c922e8ff64aaf347815f6914690b4" :edb/db-before-t 2518 :edb/db-after-t 2519 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535831 50 #inst "2026-10-09T08:22:46.212Z" 13194139535831 true] [17592186166783 1020 #edb/ref 17592186368841 13194139535831 true] [17592186167021 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$" 13194139535831 true] [17592186167021 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049128/assets/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$" 13194139535831 false] [17592186317649 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/2093298b6cacb0b29f874d401ccca40560517ff5887763e9a2b88bf4bc051abb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\ (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(1+x)^{3} & =t+K \\\\ (1+x)^{3} & =3t+C \\\\ 1+x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1.\n$$" 13194139535831 true] [17592186317649 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/2093298b6cacb0b29f874d401ccca40560517ff5887763e9a2b88bf4bc051abb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\ (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(1+x)^{3} & =t+K \\\\ (1+x)^{3} & =3t+C \\\\ 1+x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1.\n$$" 13194139535831 false] [17592186317670 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$" 13194139535831 true] [17592186317670 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$" 13194139535831 false] [17592186317684 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/466c0aa389beb6d1a42092b73bae75508ef2c50f1f08cca5efaac20d25615f30.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4(1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4(x+{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-4)^{2} & =4(1+{1}^{2})+C \\\\ 8 & =8+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8(x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\n$$" 13194139535831 true] [17592186317684 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/466c0aa389beb6d1a42092b73bae75508ef2c50f1f08cca5efaac20d25615f30.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4(1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4(x+{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-4)^{2} & =4(1+{1}^{2})+C \\\\ 8 & =8+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8(x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\n$$" 13194139535831 false] [17592186368841 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368842 13194139535831 true] [17592186368841 1089 "q-122346" 13194139535831 true] [17592186368841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535831 true] [17592186368841 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2+\\frac{x}{2}){m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=5\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139535831 true] [17592186368841 1113 #uuid "c26a753a-70e5-5794-8f64-38b83165524c" 13194139535831 true] [17592186368841 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070816/assets/d9482b116bad201dec2c71f173bf18d7dc2f170acbb37052151bba38a7898450.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+\\frac{x}{2}){m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+\\frac{x}{2})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=5\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(5)^{2} & =2(0)+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+C \\\\ \\frac{25}{2} & =0+0+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{25}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x+25}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+4(2)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+8+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35} \\\\  & ≈5.9\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535831 true] [17592186368842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368843 13194139535831 true] [17592186368842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368844 13194139535831 true] [17592186368842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368845 13194139535831 true] [17592186368842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368846 13194139535831 true] [17592186368842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368847 13194139535831 true] [17592186368842 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535831 true] [17592186368842 1041 "selection" 13194139535831 true] [17592186368842 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368843 13194139535831 true] [17592186368842 1110 #uuid "14e1cda8-c907-5e47-829e-e1a789c7dcd6" 13194139535831 true] [17592186368843 1034 "5.9\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139535831 true] [17592186368843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535831 true] [17592186368843 1111 #uuid "9f9ada89-980c-5636-83a3-b984960f1858" 13194139535831 true] [17592186368844 1034 "5.1\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139535831 true] [17592186368844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535831 true] [17592186368844 1111 #uuid "d6b5c0df-ae0d-561c-9879-2af4bc986d8f" 13194139535831 true] [17592186368845 1034 "6.8\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139535831 true] [17592186368845 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535831 true] [17592186368845 1111 #uuid "9